            <section class="research-hero">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">Computational Materials · Quantum Technologies</p><h1>Point Defects in Cubic BeS</h1><p class="subtitle">Electronic Structure and Optical Excitations</p><p class="hero-intro">First-principles research examining how vacancies and substitutional defects change cubic beryllium sulfide, with a focus on candidate spin systems for quantum technologies.</p><div class="research-meta"><span>Tuskegee University</span><span>Jan. 2024 – Mar. 2025</span><span>Undergraduate research</span></div><p class="status">Research collaboration with the University of Central Florida</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#contributions">My contributions ↓</a><a class="button" href="/documents/research/bes-defects-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open research poster ↗</a></div></div>
                <figure class="structure-hero"><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/beryllium-vacancy.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: A local change in the crystal"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/beryllium-vacancy.jpeg" alt="Crystal visualization of a beryllium vacancy in cubic BeS" width="354" height="333" /></a><figcaption><strong>A local change in the crystal</strong><span>Beryllium-vacancy structure from the research poster.</span></figcaption></figure>
