            <nav class="page-index" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#question">Research question</a><a href="#contributions">My work</a><a href="#methods">Methods</a><a href="#findings">Findings</a><a href="#poster">Poster</a></nav>
            <section class="section split" id="question"><div><p class="eyebrow">01</p><h2>Defects as a Materials Design Tool</h2></div><div class="copy"><p class="lead">Can a local defect in cubic BeS support a useful spin state and optical response?</p><p>Our study examined a beryllium vacancy and two configurations combining that vacancy with substitution on a sulfur site. We compared their electronic and optical behavior with pristine cubic BeS.</p><p>The work tested a hypothesis linking under-coordinated electronegative atoms and an even change in electron count to the formation of triplet ground states. This provided a way to select defect configurations for computational investigation.</p></div></section>
            <section class="section dark" id="contributions"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02</p><h2>My Contributions</h2></div><p>I worked on computational analysis and interpretation within a collaborative research project.</p></div><div class="contribution-list"><div><span>01</span><p>Applied DFT, GW, and Bethe–Salpeter methods to investigate pristine and defected cubic BeS.</p></div><div><span>02</span><p>Analyzed density of states, optical excitation spectra, structural stability, and local lattice distortions across the defect configurations.</p></div><div><span>03</span><p>Used computational data analysis, Fortran, and crystal visualization to interpret the results and connect atomic structure to electronic behavior.</p></div><div><span>04</span><p>Contributed to the defect-selection hypothesis and presented the collaborative findings at the APS March Meeting 2025.</p></div></div></section>
            <section class="section" id="methods"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03</p><h2>Structure, Electrons, and Light</h2></div><p>The study combined ground-state electronic-structure calculations with methods for quasiparticle and optical excitations.</p></div><div class="method-grid"><article><p class="step-number">DFT</p><h3>Ground-state structure</h3><p>Investigate the crystal and its defect configurations, including local bonding and ground-state electronic behavior.</p></article><article><p class="step-number">GW</p><h3>Electronic excitations</h3><p>Examine quasiparticle electronic structure and the density of states associated with the defect systems.</p></article><article><p class="step-number">BSE</p><h3>Optical response</h3><p>Evaluate optical excitations and dielectric response to assess how the defects interact with light.</p></article></div><div class="structure-grid" style="margin-top: 3rem"><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/beryllium-vacancy.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Beryllium vacancy"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/beryllium-vacancy.jpeg" alt="Atomic visualization of cubic BeS with a beryllium vacancy" width="354" height="333" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Beryllium vacancy</strong><span>V<sub>Be</sub>: a missing beryllium atom.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/oxygen-defect.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Vacancy with oxygen"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/oxygen-defect.jpeg" alt="Atomic visualization of a beryllium vacancy with oxygen substitution in cubic BeS" width="355" height="333" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Vacancy with oxygen</strong><span>V<sub>Be</sub>O<sub>S</sub>: a vacancy combined with oxygen substitution on a sulfur site.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/selenium-defect.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Vacancy with selenium"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/selenium-defect.jpeg" alt="Atomic visualization of a beryllium vacancy with selenium substitution in cubic BeS" width="355" height="355" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Vacancy with selenium</strong><span>V<sub>Be</sub>Se<sub>S</sub>: a vacancy combined with selenium substitution on a sulfur site.</span></figcaption></figure></div><p class="media-note">Atomic visualizations are extracted from the research poster. Its legend identifies beryllium in grey, sulfur in yellow, and atoms of interest in green.</p></section>
            <section class="section" id="findings"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04</p><h2>Reported Computational Findings</h2></div><p>The poster reports triplet ground states and dynamical stability for all three studied defect configurations.</p></div><div class="finding-grid"><article><span class="finding-number">3 defects</span><h3>Triplet ground states</h3><p>Each studied configuration exhibited a triplet ground state, consistent with the selection hypothesis used in the investigation.</p></article><article><span class="finding-number">~0.5 eV</span><h3>Low-energy optical excitations</h3><p>The defect systems showed intense optical excitations at low energies, supporting their investigation as potential spin-qubit candidates.</p></article></div><div class="spectrum-gallery"><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/defect-dos.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Spin-resolved density of states"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/defect-dos.jpeg" alt="Three original spin-up and spin-down density-of-states plots for the studied BeS defects" width="469" height="1323" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Spin-resolved density of states</strong><span>Poster plots for the vacancy, oxygen-modified, and selenium-modified defects, in that order.</span></figcaption></figure><figure class=""><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/optical-excitations.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Optical excitation spectra"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/optical-excitations.jpeg" alt="Three original optical-excitation and imaginary-dielectric-function plots for the studied BeS defects" width="587" height="1314" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Optical excitation spectra</strong><span>Original oscillator-strength and dielectric-response plots for the same three configurations.</span></figcaption></figure></div><div class="callout"><strong>Research implication</strong><p>These computational results identify promising defect candidates. Establishing the corresponding singlet states and a complete spin-initialization pathway remained further work in the poster.</p></div></section>
            <section class="section" id="poster"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">05</p><h2>Research Presentation</h2></div><p>The poster brings the crystal structures, density-of-states plots, and optical spectra together in the context of the study.</p></div><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="/documents/research/bes-defects-poster.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View the full poster ↗</a><a class="button" href="/documents/research/bes-defects-poster.pdf" download>Download PDF ↓</a></div><figure class="poster-preview"><a href="/images/research/bes-defects/poster-preview.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" aria-label="Open full figure: Research poster"><img src="/images/research/bes-defects/poster-preview.png" alt="Full research poster on triplet ground states and optical excitations of local defects in cubic BeS" width="2200" height="1700" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption><strong>Research poster</strong><span>Presented at the APS March Meeting 2025 in Anaheim, California. Open the PDF for full-resolution figures.</span></figcaption></figure><p class="credits"><strong>Poster authors:</strong> Daniel Winfield, Sergey Stolbov, and Marisol Alcántara Ortigoza. Tuskegee University and the University of Central Florida. Supported by the U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Science, Basic Energy Sciences, under Award DE-SC0024487.</p></section>

